@node Lexical Elements
@section Lexical Elements
The Pascal frontend recognizes the following lexical tokens: numbers, string literals, identifiers, operators, separators, and label definitions. Whitespace (spaces, tabs, carriage returns, line feeds) is ignored except to separate tokens. Comments are not supported in this frontend.

@node Labels
@section Labels
Labels are defined by an identifier or a number followed by a colon (@code{:}). They are used to mark positions in the generated assembly code for branching or subroutine calls. The lexer converts patterns like @code{Identifier:} or @code{Number:} into special @code{:label} tokens, which the parser translates into assembly entry nodes. Labels have no runtime value; they solely affect the placement of generated code.

Example:
@example
Start:
   MOVE 5 Counter
   Goto Start
@end example

@node Constants
@section Constants
Constants include numeric literals and string literals. Numeric literals support multiple formats:

@table @asis
@item Decimal
Standard base-10 integers (e.g., @code{42}, @code{0}).

@item Hexadecimal
Prefixed with @code{0x} or @code{0X} (e.g., @code{0xFF}, @code{0xDEADBEEF}). Values are case-insensitive for hex digits.

@item Octal
Prefixed with @code{0o} or @code{0O} (e.g., @code{0o755}, @code{0o17}). Digits must be in range 0–7.

@item Binary
Prefixed with @code{0b} or @code{0B} (e.g., @code{0b1101}, @code{0b11110000}). Digits must be 0 or 1.

@item DWORD (Pascal-style)
Two formats:
@itemize @bullet
@item @code{D'value'} (e.g., @code{D'255'}) — decimal value between quotes.
@item @code{&Hvalue} (e.g., @code{&HFF}) — hexadecimal value with &H prefix.
@end itemize

All numeric literals are internally converted to their decimal equivalents during lexical analysis.
@end table

String literals are enclosed in double quotes (@code{"..."}) and may contain any characters except the quote itself; the quotes are not part of the string value. There is no support for escape sequences within strings.

Examples:
@example
Counter := 42              ; Decimal literal
RegValue := 0xFF           ; Hexadecimal (255 decimal)
Permission := 0o644        ; Octal (420 decimal)
Mask := 0b11110000        ; Binary (240 decimal)
Size := D'512'             ; DWORD decimal format
Color := &HFF0000          ; DWORD hex format
Message := "Hello, world!"
@end example

@node Identifiers
@section Identifiers
Identifiers are sequences of letters, digits, and underscores, beginning with a letter or underscore. They are case-insensitive: the lexer normalizes all identifiers to @strong{PascalCase} (first letter uppercase, remaining letters lowercase) before producing AST nodes. This ensures that @code{myVariable}, @code{MYVARIABLE}, and @code{MyVariable} all refer to the same identifier @code{Myvariable} in the generated code.

Examples:
@example
playerScore     → Playerscore
PLAYER_SCORE    → Player_score
PlayerScore     → Playerscore
_internalFlag   → _internalflag
@end example

This normalization simplifies code generation and avoids subtle bugs caused by case mismatches while allowing programmers to write identifiers in their preferred style.

@node Operators
@section Operators
The frontend supports arithmetic, relational, logical, and unary operators. Arithmetic operators: @code{+} (addition), @code{-} (subtraction), @code{*} (multiplication), @code{/} (division). Relational operators: @code{=} (equality), @code{<>} (inequality), @code{<} (less than), @code{>} (greater than), @code{<=} (less than or equal), @code{>=} (greater than or equal). Logical operators: @code{AND} (conjunction), @code{OR} (disjunction), @code{NOT} (negation). The unary minus operator (@code{-}) negates an expression.

@example
Total := Price * Quantity
Discount := Total * 0.1
if Price > 100 and not Special then ...
@end example

@node Separators
@section Separators
Separators structure the program: semicolon (@code{;}) terminates statements, comma (@code{,}) separates argument lists, colon (@code{:}) defines labels, parentheses (@code{( } and @code{ )}) group expressions and delimit parameter lists, and dot (@code{.}) appears in the grammar but has no specific meaning in this frontend.

@example
Procedure Foo;
   Move 1 X, 2 Y;
end
@end example

@node Routine Declarations
@section Routine Declarations
Routines are declared using the @code{PROCEDURE} keyword. A procedure may be standalone or associated with a class via the @code{ON} keyword. The procedure header is followed by a @code{BEGIN}...@code{END} block containing statements. An empty procedure body is allowed.

Example of a standalone procedure:
@example
PROCEDURE Initialize
   MOVE 0 Score
end
@end example

Example of a class method:
@example
PROCEDURE PlayerClass.Move
   MOVE X + DX FX
end
@end example

@node Statements
@section Statements
Statements perform actions and are terminated by semicolons. The frontend supports label statements, assignments, routine calls, conditionals, loops, exit statements, error reporting, and copy directives.

@node Label Statements
@subsection Label Statements
A label statement consists of a label identifier or number followed by a colon. It serves only as a target for jumps and does not affect program state.

@example
LoopStart:
   ...
   IF Condition GOTO LoopStart
@end example

@node Assignment Statements
@subsection Assignment Statements
Assignments store the result of an expression into a variable. Two syntaxes are supported: the traditional @code{Variable := Expression} and the @code{MOVE Expression Variable} form (reminiscent of COBOL). Both are semantically identical.

@example
Counter := Counter + 1
MOVE Total Price
@end example

@node Call Statements
@subsection Call Statements
Routines are invoked with the @code{CALL} keyword. Variations include:
@itemize @bullet
@item @code{CALL RoutineName} — direct call to a standalone routine or class method.
@item @code{CALL MethodName ON ObjectName} — invoke a method on an object instance.
@item @code{CALL RoutineName IN BankNumber} — call a routine located in a specific memory bank.
@item @code{CALL RoutineName IN LIBRARY} — call a library routine (bank ignored).
@end itemize

Examples:
@example
CALL Initialize
CALL DrawSprite ON Player
CALL LoadLevel IN 3
CALL SaveGame IN LIBRARY
@end example

@node Conditional Statements
@subsection Conditional Statements
The @code{IF}@code{THEN}@code{ELSE} construct enables conditional execution. The @code{ELSE} clause is optional. The condition is an expression that evaluates to a boolean value (true/false). Execution proceeds to the @code{THEN} block if true, otherwise to the @code{ELSE} block (if present).

@example
IF Score > 1000 THEN
   Status := "HIGH SCORE"
ELSE
   Status := "PLAYING"
END IF
@end example

@node Loop Statements
@subsection Loop Statements
Loops repeat a block of statements while a condition holds. Five forms are supported:
@itemize @bullet
@item @code{WHILE Condition NEXT StatementList} — pre-test loop.
@item @code{UNTIL Condition NEXT StatementList} — post‑test loop (loop until condition true).
@item @code{DO NEXT WHILE Condition StatementList} — equivalent to WHILE.
@item @code{DO NEXT UNTIL Condition StatementList} — equivalent to UNTIL.
@item @code{FOR Variable FROM Start TO End STEP Step NEXT StatementList} — numeric loop with explicit increment.
@end itemize

Examples:
@example
WHILE Lives > 0 NEXT
   CALL ProcessInput
END WHILE

UNTIL GameOver NEXT
   CALL UpdateGame
END UNTIL

FOR I FROM 1 TO 10 STEP 1 NEXT
   MOVE I Score
END FOR
@end example

@node Exit Statements
@subsection Exit Statements
The @code{EXIT} statement terminates the current method and returns control to its caller. The @code{GOBACK} statement is synonymous with @code{EXIT} in this frontend.

@example
EXIT
GOBACK
@end example

@node Error Statements
@subsection Error Statements
Error reporting is performed with @code{LOG FAULT Code}, which logs a numeric fault code for debugging. The @code{STOP Code} statement triggers a debugger break with the given code.

@example
LOG FAULT 1000
STOP $FF
@end example

@node Copy Statements
@subsection Copy Statements
The @code{COPY} directive includes external source files during compilation. It takes a string literal naming the file to copy.

@example
COPY "utils.inc"
@end example

@node Expressions
@section Expressions
Expressions compute values from identifiers, constants, and operators. They obey standard precedence: unary minus highest, then multiplication and division, then addition and subtraction, then relational operators, then logical AND, finally logical OR. Parentheses may override precedence.

An expression is either a primary (identifier, number, string, or parenthesized expression), a unary operator applied to an expression, or a binary operator combining two expressions.

@example
(FX + DX) * 2
NOT (Flag AND Active)
Price < 100 OR Discount > 0
@end example

@node Functions
@section Functions
The Pascal frontend itself provides several functions for lexical analysis and parsing, usable only within the compiler internals.

@node process-labels
@subsection process-labels
Converts sequences of tokens where an identifier or number is followed by a colon into explicit label tokens. Input: a list of raw tokens from the lexer. Output: a new token list with label tokens substituted. Side‑effect: none; the function is purely functional.

@node pascal-lex-line
@subsection pascal-lex-line
Lexes a single line of source text into a list of (type, value) token pairs. Handles numbers, strings, identifiers, operators, separators, and begins label processing. Input: a string line. Output: token list for that line. Side‑effect: none.

@node pascal-lex-source
@subsection pascal-lex-source
Lexes an entire source string by concatenating the results of @code{pascal-lex-line} for each non‑empty, non‑comment line. Input: complete source string. Output: flat token list for the whole source. Side‑effect: none.

@node pascal-tokenize
@subsection pascal-tokenize
Alias for @code{pascal-lex-source}; provided for interface compatibility. Input: source string. Output: token list. Side‑effect: none.

@node pascal-parse
@subsection pascal-parse
Parses a token list into an abstract syntax tree (AST) representing the program. Input: token list from the lexer. Output: AST node tree (typically a program node). Side‑effect: builds AST structures via internal constructor functions.

@node pascal-binary-operator
@subsection pascal-binary-operator
Maps a binary operator token (e.g., @code{:plus}) and two sub‑expressions into a standard EIGHTBOL expression node (addition, subtraction, etc.). Input: operator keyword, left expression, right expression. Output: expression node. Side‑effect: none.

@node pascal-unary-operator
@subsection pascal-unary-operator
Maps a unary operator token (@code{:not} or @code{:unary-minus}) and an operand expression into a standard EIGHTBOL expression node (logical negation or arithmetic negation). Input: operator keyword, operand expression. Output: expression node. Side‑effect: none.

@node make-debug-break-node
@subsection make-debug-break-node
Creates a debug‑break AST node used by the @code{STOP} statement. Input: numeric code value. Output: AST node representing a debug break. Side‑effect: none.

@node normalize-identifier
@subsection normalize-identifier
Normalizes an identifier NAME to PascalCase (first letter uppercase, remaining letters lowercase). All identifiers are normalized during lexical analysis to ensure case-insensitive matching and consistent code generation. Input: identifier string (any case). Output: PascalCase string. Side‑effect: none.

Examples:
@example
(normalize-identifier "myVariable") → "Myvariable"
(normalize-identifier "MYVAR") → "Myvar"
(normalize-identifier "MyVar") → "Myvar"
@end example

@node pascal-token-type
@subsection pascal-token-type
Classifies a lexeme string as @code{:number}, @code{:string}, or @code{:ident}. Recognizes all number literal formats including decimal, hexadecimal (0x), octal (0o), binary (0b), and DWORD formats (D'...' and &H). Input: string lexeme. Output: token type keyword. Side‑effect: none.

Examples:
@example
(pascal-token-type "42") → :number
(pascal-token-type "0xFF") → :number
(pascal-token-type "0o755") → :number
(pascal-token-type "MyVar") → :ident
(pascal-token-type "\"text\"") → :string
@end example

@node parse-pascal-number
@subsection parse-pascal-number
Parses a numeric lexeme with various bases and formats, returning the decimal equivalent. Supports decimal, hexadecimal (0x), octal (0o), binary (0b), and DWORD formats (D'...' and &H). Invalid formats fallback to decimal parsing. Input: numeric string lexeme. Output: integer value in decimal. Side‑effect: none.

Examples:
@example
(parse-pascal-number "0xFF") → 255
(parse-pascal-number "0o755") → 493
(parse-pascal-number "0b11010110") → 214
(parse-pascal-number "D'255'") → 255
(parse-pascal-number "&HFF") → 255
@end example

@node pascal-convert-lexer-tokens
@subsection pascal-convert-lexer-tokens
Transforms lexer output tokens into YACC-compatible format. Maps keyword identifiers to their corresponding token types and passes through numeric, string, and operator tokens. Input: list of lexer tokens (pairs of type and value). Output: list of YACC-compatible token tuples. Side‑effect: none.

@node Analogies to Reader Macros
@section Number Literal Parsing
The Pascal frontend supports multiple number literal formats to enhance code readability and compatibility:

@itemize @bullet
@item Hexadecimal notation (@code{0xFF}, @code{0x1A2B}) is useful for bit patterns and memory addresses.
@item Octal notation (@code{0o755}) suits Unix file permissions and traditional octal constants.
@item Binary notation (@code{0b11010110}) is ideal for bit-field and flag definitions.
@item DWORD formats (@code{D'255'}, @code{&HFF}) provide compatibility with legacy Pascal code.
@end itemize

All number formats are parsed during lexical analysis and converted to their decimal equivalents before AST production. This means that @code{0xFF}, @code{255}, and @code{D'255'} all represent the same value in the generated code.

@example
; Hexadecimal $FF is written as 0xFF
MOVE 0xFF Register

; Binary masks for clarity
Flag1 := 0b00000001
Flag2 := 0b00000010
Mask := Flag1 OR Flag2

; Octal for Unix permissions
FileMode := 0o755
@end example

